    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In Japan, workers are mainly employed through two different systems: traditional membership-style employment, characterized by lifetime commitment and seniority-based promotions, and Western job-style employment, which focuses on workers’ expertise and performance-based advancement. Today, membership-style employment is increasingly viewed as socially unsustainable and the rising controversy demands an alternative like job-style employment. I explore the complexity of the school-to-work transition in Japan, investigating whether the academic abilities of recent graduates in Japan differ by their employment style. Applying a theoretical framework incorporating university massification, signaling theory, and human capital theory, I conducted an OLS regression analysis using the Programme for the International Assessment of Adult Competencies (PIAAC) 2013. The results indicate that university graduates’ academic ability is statistically significantly lower for those employed through membership-style employment relative to job-style employment. This finding stimulates further debate on Japan’s optimum employment system and the social value of higher education in the 21st century.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Yamauchi, M. (2024). Membership-Style vs. Job-Style Employment: The Relationship between Traditional Employment Practices and the Academic Ability of Recent Graduates in Japan. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/ty330vg2304. https://doi.org/10.25740/ty330vg2304.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
